<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trans-Indochinese Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as North–South railway (Vietnam), Transindochinois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trans-Indochinese Railway is the metre-gauge line of about 1,700 kilometres running along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, now Ho Chi Minh City. Begun under Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-doumer">Paul Doumer</a> in the 1890s and built in sections, it was completed in 1936. Heavily damaged during the wars of the following decades, it was reopened along its full length in 1976 as the North–South Railway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trans-indochinese-railway.jpg" alt="Trans-Indochinese Railway" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilrad rom · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VietnamRailway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1895 the outgoing Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>, Jean Marie de Lanessan, urged his successors to give priority to a line between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, which he called the backbone of Indochina. Paul Doumer, appointed in 1897, proposed a railway programme that included this line and what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>–Vietnam Railway, and the French government approved a loan of 200 million francs. The Hanoi–Vinh section was built from 1899 to 1905 and the Nha Trang–Saigon section from 1905 to 1913, with lines around Huế opened in 1906 and 1908. Vinh–Huế followed between 1913 and 1927 and Huế–Nha Trang between 1930 and 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The first through journeys on the Transindochinois took about 60 hours, cut to about 40 by the late 1930s, behind French Pacific or Mikado locomotives with dining and sleeping cars. Japanese forces used the line heavily in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, drawing sabotage by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> and American bombing. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> the French ran the armoured train La Rafale from February 1951, but the Viet Minh kept lifting rails and built a network of 300 km of their own between Ninh Hoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-nang">Da Nang</a>.</p><p>After the Geneva Accords of 1954 the line was cut at the Hiền Lương Bridge over the Bến Hải River. South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, with American support, rebuilt the track between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and Huế in the late 1950s, but 795 attacks between 1961 and 1964 forced it to abandon long sections. In the North, American raids struck railway bridges, among them the Thanh Hóa Bridge, which withstood repeated attacks from April 1965 until laser-guided bombs destroyed it in April and May 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 30 April 1975 the line was restored and reopened on 31 December 1976 as a symbol of Vietnamese unity; 1,334 bridges, 27 tunnels and 158 stations were repaired, and other lines such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-lat">Da Lat</a>–Thap Cham Railway were dismantled for materials. It remains a single-track line operated by Vietnam Railways, the main railway of the country, and its trains are sometimes called the Reunification Express, though no train carries that name officially.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North–South railway (Vietnam)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony transformed the region’s infrastructure: the Haiphong–Kunming railway to Yunnan was completed in 1910, the Long Bien bridge across the Red River at Hanoi in 1902, and the <b>Trans-Indochinese Railway</b> from Hanoi to Saigon in 1936.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
